::ALG2_U03_L04_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup> - 9)/(x - 3). {
= x + 3, x &ne; 3#Correct. Factor x<sup>2</sup> - 9 as (x - 3)(x + 3), then keep the original restriction x &ne; 3.
~ x - 3, x &ne; 3#The remaining factor after canceling is x + 3.
~ x + 3, no restriction#The original denominator still gives x &ne; 3.
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 3, x &ne; 3#The numerator must be factored before canceling.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>What restriction applies to (x + 4)/(x - 7)? {
~ x &ne; -4#The numerator does not create a restriction.
= x &ne; 7#Correct. The denominator x - 7 cannot equal 0.
~ x &ne; -7#The sign is reversed.
~ all real numbers#x = 7 makes the denominator 0.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (6x<sup>2</sup>)/(3x), with x &ne; 0. {
~ 2x<sup>2</sup>#One factor of x cancels.
~ 3x#The coefficients simplify to 2, not 3.
= 2x#Correct. Divide coefficients and subtract exponents: 6x<sup>2</sup>/(3x) = 2x.
~ 2/x#The remaining x stays in the numerator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Multiply (2x/5)(10/x<sup>2</sup>). {
~ 4x#One x remains in the denominator.
~ 2/x, x &ne; 0#The coefficient simplifies to 4, not 2.
~ 4/x, no restriction#The original denominator includes x<sup>2</sup>.
= 4/x, x &ne; 0#Correct. The product is 20x/(5x<sup>2</sup>) = 4/x, and x cannot be 0.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x<sup>2</sup> - 4)/(x + 1) by (x - 2)/(x + 1). {
= x + 2, x &ne; -1 and x &ne; 2#Correct. Multiply by the reciprocal; factors x - 2 and x + 1 cancel, but original restrictions remain.
~ x - 2#The remaining factor is x + 2.
~ x + 2, no restriction#Restrictions from original denominators and divisor must be kept.
~ 1/(x + 2)#This flips the wrong expression.
}

